PR #661  overall: potential_risk
Spec: ACLIC spec previously implied MMIO interface by calling ACLIC 'a tailored APLIC,' risking that implementers would not apply CSR-level access controls to interrupt controller configuration, enabling lower-privilege code to manipulate interrupt routing and escalate privileges.
  [OK] include/sbi/riscv_encoding.h:759-765  CSR_MISELECT / CSR_MIREG macros
      Pure constant definitions matching the RISC-V AIA spec. No access-control logic resides here. These CSR numbers are the spec-defined access portals shared between IMSIC and ACLIC; the security concern is about how downstream code uses them, not their definition.
  [OK] include/sbi/riscv_encoding.h:369-375  CSR_SISELECT / CSR_SIREG macros
      Pure constant definitions for S-mode indirect CSR alias registers. Same as M-mode: the constants themselves are spec-compliant; access-control concerns arise only when code programs these CSRs without range gating.
  [OK] include/sbi/riscv_encoding.h:451-457  CSR_VSISELECT / CSR_VSIREG macros
      Pure constant definitions for VS-mode indirect CSR alias registers. Completes the M/S/VS privilege-level picture. Constants themselves are correct per the AIA spec; risk is downstream usage without per-selector-range access differentiation.
  [RISK] lib/utils/irqchip/imsic.c:57-81  imsic_csr_write / imsic_csr_read / imsic_csr_set / imsic_csr_clear
      These macros implement the canonical indirect CSR access pattern (write MISELECT, then operate on MIREG) with NO *iselect range validation. Any caller can write any value to MISELECT. Currently safe because only IMSIC code uses these macros, but the codebase relies on an implicit invariant — 'only IMSIC selects are ever written to MISELECT' — that is NOT enforced locally. If ACLIC code were added reusing MISELECT/MIREG for ACLIC-specific selector ranges, these macros would become a direct access-control bypass vector, since CSRs bypass PMP entirely.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:89-104  mstatus_init
      Smstateen configuration is a coarse all-or-nothing gate: if SBI_HART_EXT_SMAIA is detected, SMSTATEEN0_AIA, SMSTATEEN0_IMSIC, and SMSTATEEN0_SVSLCT are all enabled for lower-privilege modes unconditionally. No per-selector-range granularity exists. If Smaclic/Ssaclic were added, an ACLIC-specific Smstateen bit (distinct from SMSTATEEN0_IMSIC) would need to be introduced here to separately gate ACLIC-specific *iselect ranges. The current code has no provision for such a split, depending on the implicit assumption that IMSIC is the only consumer of the AIA indirect-CSR interface.
  [RISK] include/sbi/sbi_hart.h:30-31  enum sbi_hart_extensions
      SBI_HART_EXT_SMAIA is the sole enum value gating all AIA M-mode CSR functionality. No SBI_HART_EXT_SMACLIC or SBI_HART_EXT_SSACLIC exists. The extension detection model treats the entire AIA indirect-CSR selector space as a monolithic property, with no framework for detecting or gating ACLIC-specific subsets. Adding Smaclic/Ssaclic would require extending this enum and all sites that branch on SBI_HART_EXT_SMAIA to distinguish ACLIC-specific functionality from IMSIC.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:131-146  switch_to_next_domain_context
      Domain context switch saves/restores 13 S-mode CSRs (sstatus, sie, stvec, sscratch, sepc, scause, stval, sip, satp, scounteren, senvcfg, srmcfg) plus trap state, but does NOT save/restore SISELECT, SIREG, or SIREG2-6. The struct hart_context (lines 24-59) has no fields for these registers. If Ssaclic configuration were programmed through SISELECT/SIREG and two domains had different interrupt routing/delegation/priority settings, a domain switch would leak the previous domain's ACLIC state to the next domain — a cross-domain information disclosure via leftover indirect CSR state.
  [RISK] lib/utils/irqchip/imsic.c:389-399  imsic_cold_irqchip_init
      Adds IMSIC MMIO regions to root domain via sbi_domain_root_add_memrange() with SBI_DOMAIN_MEMREGION_MMIO for PMP-based protection (line 394). However, IMSIC registers are simultaneously accessible through CSR_MISELECT/CSR_MIREG without any equivalent CSR-access gating. This dual-path access — MMIO path gated by PMP, CSR path gated only by the coarse SMSTATEEN bits in mstatus_init — instantiates the exact access-control mismatch the ACLIC spec change warns against. If ACLIC were added following this template, it would inherit the same gap: PMP protects the MMIO window while the CSR window offers an un-gated side channel.
  [OK] lib/utils/irqchip/imsic.c:206-229  imsic_local_eix_update
      Computes isel values targeting IMSIC-specific selector ranges (IMSIC_EIP0/IMSIC_EIE0). The address computation is IMSIC-domain-specific; it would not accidentally address ACLIC selectors. The function correctly batches per-XLEN-bit groups into single ireg writes. No access-control bypass here — the isel values are bounded within IMSIC's known selector space by construction.
  [RISK] lib/utils/irqchip/imsic.c:259-277  imsic_warm_irqchip_init
      Per-hart warm init disables all IMSIC interrupts via imsic_local_eix_update, then calls imsic_local_irqchip_init. Both use the imsic_csr_* macros which write CSR_MISELECT as a shared resource. If ACLIC were a separate irqchip_device sharing CSR_MISELECT/CSR_MIREG, this warm-init path and an ACLIC warm-init path would race for MISELECT — there is no spinlock, no per-hart mutual exclusion, and the MISELECT register is a single register shared across all indirect-CSR-accessed controllers. A concurrent ACLIC init could observe a MISELECT value written by IMSIC (or vice versa), causing writes to target the wrong controller's register.
  [OK] lib/utils/irqchip/imsic.c:231-257  imsic_local_irqchip_init
      Checks Smaia availability via csr_read_allowed(CSR_MTOPI) (a clean trap-based probe), then configures IMSIC eithreshold/eidelivery through the standard imsic_csr_write pattern. This is conventional IMSIC init using the indirect CSR mechanism. No security violation in isolation — the function correctly gates on Smaia presence and only touches IMSIC's own selector space.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_trap.c:317-322  sbi_trap_handler
      Interrupt dispatch is a binary branch: sbi_trap_aia_irq() vs. sbi_trap_nonaia_irq(), gated solely by SBI_HART_EXT_SMAIA (line 318-319). If Smaclic introduces new interrupt sources (e.g., hart-local wired interrupts routed via ACLIC) that appear through the AIA TOPI facility but require different handling than IMSIC-originated interrupts, this single dispatch point would need Smaclic awareness. The current binary dispatch assumes all AIA TOPI-reported interrupts are IMSIC file interrupts, but ACLIC could inject interrupt identities into the same TOPI reporting channel.

